WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.500
- A lot of your favorite
RNB and dance tunes

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.240
have killer background vocals

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.280
that you might not have
noticed on your first listen.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.010
Just check out the harmonies in the chorus

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.231
of Ariana Grande's "Thank U, Next."

00:00:10.231 --> 00:00:11.783
♪ Thank u, next ♪

00:00:11.783 --> 00:00:12.616
♪ Next ♪

00:00:12.616 --> 00:00:13.820
♪ Thank u ♪

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.980
In this episode, we're going to dive

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.060
into the world of background vocals,

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.560
and I'm even gonna break
down how I write vocals

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.232
for my own music.

00:00:22.232 --> 00:00:25.260
♪ Ah, ah, ah ♪

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.500
I have been a musician
pretty much my whole life,

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.780
but I never intended to be
a professional musician.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:33.960
And by that, I mean I never thought

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.720
that I could even really
afford to have a music career

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.880
and have that be the thing
that I do for my job.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.410
(people laughing)

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.750
Music was always a constant in my life.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.533
It's interesting to me now.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.730
I wouldn't have assumed that
I would become an RnB singer.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:52.920
And I think a big part of
that is going to Oberlin,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.930
which is where my music
career really started.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.150
I was asked to perform in an RnB showcase,

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.030
and I sang a couple of Kehlani songs.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:00.900
I was a huge Kehlani fan.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.970
And "No scrubs," which I still perform

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:05.472
at almost all my shows. (laughs)

00:01:05.472 --> 00:01:07.540
♪ Hanging out the passenger side ♪

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:10.450
And I think that experience
really opened my eyes

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.980
because I was able to see
that I could be an artist

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.980
and I could be an artist in this field

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.940
without having the music experience

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:20.800
that I was taught I needed to have.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.580
This next song is called
"Green Tea Ice Cream."

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.500
One of my songs that has
backing vocals that I love

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.580
is "Green Tea Ice Cream."

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.730
The sounds that I came up with

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.960
just felt like they belonged there.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.450
And I didn't want another
instrument to play that part,

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.655
so I made my voice the
instrument to play the part.

00:01:37.655 --> 00:01:38.550
♪ By the sea ♪

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.059
♪ Sweatshirts sweatpants ♪

00:01:40.059 --> 00:01:41.280
♪ White noise blue jeans ♪

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.640
Vocal layering has been
a musical tradition

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.510
of cultures around the globe
for thousands of years.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:49.970
Prehistoric hunters in Africa and Asia

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:52.300
are known to have used polyphonic singing,

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.060
which is where individual vocalists

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.910
sing unique phrases and notes together.

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:59.760
In ancient Greece, a chorus
was a group of singers

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.620
that would describe the
action of a play through song.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:04.440
And during the Middle Ages,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.760
organum described one or more voices

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.870
accompanying a main melody chant.

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:12.710
Today, backing vocals are an
essential part of popular music

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:15.730
and are used to give
tracks energy and depth.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.120
Background vocals in genres like RnB

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.270
often include two or more layers
of the same note in unison

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.780
in order to give the
vocals a fuller effect.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.390
This is often referred to as doubling.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.680
You can hear this in the
verse of "What You Did,"

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.429
where the second half
of each line is doubled.

00:02:31.429 --> 00:02:33.385
♪ And I know you know ♪

00:02:33.385 --> 00:02:36.850
♪ I've been holding guard
for so long, so long ♪

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:40.010
And in rap music, lyricists
often use doubled vocals

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.387
to highlight keywords at
the end of each phrase.

00:02:42.387 --> 00:02:44.091
♪ Focus on the picture in front of me ♪

00:02:44.091 --> 00:02:46.900
♪ You as clear as DVD
on digital TV screens ♪

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:51.090
Vocal lyrics in the chorus or
hook often include harmonies.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.690
Unlike unison vocals,

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:55.469
harmonies use two or more
notes simultaneously.

00:02:55.469 --> 00:02:57.824
♪ Call me when you want ♪
♪ Call me when you need ♪

00:02:57.824 --> 00:03:00.214
♪ Call me in the morning
♪ ♪ I'll be on the way ♪

00:03:00.214 --> 00:03:03.560
RnB artists will sometimes
use three-part harmonies

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.959
to give the vocals the
fullness of a whole cord.

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:08.161
♪ One wants fool's love ♪

00:03:08.161 --> 00:03:11.940
♪ That kind of messy love ♪

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.910
Sometimes I think something
like a voice is a lot softer

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:17.810
than something like a brass instrument

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.390
or even a full quartet

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.270
if I think that's something beautiful.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:22.800
There's just something so vulnerable

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.400
about using your own voice.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.330
And I also think that it keeps the track

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:29.840
very light and ethereal while
still adding depth to it

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.680
and making it feel full.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.407
So here's an example
of a two-part harmony.

00:03:34.407 --> 00:03:36.740
♪ Ooh, whoa ♪

00:03:37.887 --> 00:03:40.856
And then this is an example
of a three-part harmony.

00:03:40.856 --> 00:03:41.940
♪ Ah, ah ♪

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.290
There is one line in the background vocal

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.920
like the one that I just
sang that I want to stick out

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.530
more than the rest of
the background vocal.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:51.640
So I wanna make sure that
that line stays consistent

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:54.570
so that I can lower the rest
of the background vocals

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:55.740
if that's what I need to do.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.750
So that's just all part
of texture and balance.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:02.550
♪ Ooh, whoa ♪

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.633
♪ Ah, ah ♪

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.410
That brings us to ad libs,
short for ad libitum,

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:12.130
which generally means
with freedom in Latin.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:14.910
This is where the lead
singer improvises a melody,

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.720
usually over the course of the song.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.790
For example, in "He Wasn't Man Enough,"

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:21.340
Toni Braxton is improvising new lyrics

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:22.849
on top of the original melody.

00:04:22.849 --> 00:04:25.368
♪ Do you know about us back
then? ♪ ♪ Calling me all along ♪

00:04:25.368 --> 00:04:26.965
♪ Do you know I dumped your husband ♪

00:04:26.965 --> 00:04:28.690
♪ Begging me ♪

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.700
The ad libs are clearly the lead vocal

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.210
and not the background.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:35.500
However, these days, ad libs also refer

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.270
to the improvised lines
added to hip hop tracks

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:40.120
after the main vocals are recorded.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:41.265
♪ With my Timbs on ♪

00:04:41.265 --> 00:04:42.448
♪ How many championships ♪

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:44.010
♪ What, six rings on ♪

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:45.890
This is to compliment the lead vocal

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:47.700
and even create a countermelody.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.570
The last background vocal
we have for this section

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:53.252
is an ad lib which is my favorite.

00:04:53.252 --> 00:04:55.585
♪ Ooh, whoa ♪

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.070
Ah, so good.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:01.510
Singers often add call and
response to repeated choruses

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:02.800
in order to give the section

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.552
more variety and emotional energy.

00:05:05.552 --> 00:05:06.865
♪ Like how can I ♪

00:05:06.865 --> 00:05:08.990
♪ How do I manage expectations ♪

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:10.290
So in this part of the song,

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:13.620
you can hear one of the main
uses of call and response.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.570
And the way that I used it in the song

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:17.430
is to finish the sentence.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.370
So the sentence means one thing

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:21.070
when it's just the lead vocals,

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:23.070
but then when it's the background vocals,

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:24.730
it's kind of a finished thought.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.100
When I perform "Green Tea Ice Cream" live,

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.630
I feel like it's so apparent

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:31.930
how important the background vocals are

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:33.540
because I don't always have the chance

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.770
to perform it with other singers.

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:39.420
So that is one of my songs
that if I have the chance,

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.750
I always perform it with
background vocalists.

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:43.910
Backing vocalists are singers that provide

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:47.020
background harmonies or a
countermelody to the lead singer.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:49.970
Before the invention of tape
recording in the 20th century,

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:51.830
it was impossible for the lead singer

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:54.370
to perform their own harmonies
or double their vocals

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:55.331
in a live performance.

00:05:55.331 --> 00:05:56.897
♪ Sirens in the dark ♪

00:05:56.897 --> 00:05:59.270
Those little moments are the moments

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:00.810
that I come up with first.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:01.828
You know, like the little-

00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:03.690
♪ Ooh ♪

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.270
- riffs come first

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:09.850
and then those long background
vocals and harmonies

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:13.860
that kind of tie the parts
together come second for me

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:15.980
because those are more instrumental,

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:18.950
and those kind of make me consider

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:21.677
the direction of the song
more than just this moment.

00:06:21.677 --> 00:06:23.708
I want this moment to shine.

00:06:23.708 --> 00:06:25.083
♪ Sweatshirts sweatpants ♪

00:06:25.083 --> 00:06:27.173
♪ White noise blue jeans ♪

00:06:27.173 --> 00:06:30.072
♪ Writing letters to old friends ♪

00:06:30.072 --> 00:06:33.457
♪ Black cats and tender moments ♪

00:06:33.457 --> 00:06:35.420
All the background vocals in this song

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:38.670
have an effect in the mixing process.

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:40.200
But right now they're just raw,

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.430
so it's gonna sound a
little bit differently

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:45.710
than it would sound once
the song is fully produced.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:48.620
In the pop music realm, there
are often teams of people

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:51.120
working on individual tracks or albums.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:52.610
Vocal producers are in charge

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:54.200
of coaching the recording artists

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.820
as well as arranging, editing, processing,

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.010
and mixing each take to perfection.

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:01.670
Many songwriters and singers
are capable of doing this job

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:04.650
on their own, but talented
industry favorite Kuk Harrell

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:05.950
has made a name for himself

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:08.660
by creating the background
and leading vocal magic

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:12.230
on hits songs featuring
stars like Rihanna, Beyonce,

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:13.750
and Justin Bieber.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:15.740
Something I love about background vocals

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:17.080
is that you get to say things

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:20.080
that you didn't necessarily
get to stay in the lead vocals,

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:22.010
whether that's just because it doesn't fit

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:25.080
as well with the song or
it doesn't rhyme, maybe,

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:27.100
or it doesn't fit the theme as well,

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.360
or it's just like a little secret

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:31.150
for the people that are
really listening well,

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:34.420
or maybe what you were saying
was a little bit vulnerable

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.530
and you don't wanna put
it front and center.

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:40.160
So I love RnB because
I love how RnB vocals

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:42.390
are like normal line, like-

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:43.460
♪ You wronged me ♪

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:44.715
- and then the background vocals are like-

00:07:44.715 --> 00:07:47.983
♪ I threw you things in the backyard ♪

00:07:47.983 --> 00:07:51.310
That is the kind of background vocals

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:52.510
that I was growing up on.

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:56.070
So like Beyonce did that, Kelly Rowland.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.240
They're the queens of that kind of shade

00:07:58.240 --> 00:07:59.650
and Jazmine Sullivan, you know.

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:02.960
Even the vocal riffs,
the way that you riff,

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:06.300
I feel like you can tell
someone off with a vocal riff,

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:07.420
and I have.

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:08.670
Thanks for watching.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:10.860
But before you go, we wanna hear from you.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:13.950
What's your favorite track with
beautiful background vocals?

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:16.670
Record a video of yourself
responding to this question

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:19.110
and send it to us using the
link in the description,

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.287
and you might just see
yourself in our next episode.

00:08:21.287 --> 00:08:23.704
(soft music)

